# Flutter / Android / iOS toolchain output
#
# This repository is L5 and its toolchain is Dart and Gradle, not Node — the
# generic ignore list above covers neither. `android/.gradle/` (the Gradle
# build cache, tens of megabytes and machine-specific) was showing up as
# untracked on a clean checkout, which is how a build cache ends up committed.
.gradle/
android/.gradle/
android/app/debug/
android/app/profile/
android/app/release/
android/local.properties
ios/Pods/
ios/.symlinks/
ios/Flutter/Flutter.framework/
ios/Flutter/ephemeral/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
.packages
.pub-cache/
.pub/

# One-off repair scripts and analyzer dumps must never be committed again.
# Fifteen of them were tracked at this repo's root — twelve Python/PowerShell
# fixers hardcoding paths into the retired ERPSys monorepo and a Gemini IDE task
# log, plus three UTF-16 `flutter analyze` dumps. Three had already been deleted
# once in the monorepo (see docs/ai/CHANGELOG.md) and came back through the
# polyrepo extraction, which is why this is an ignore rule rather than a cleanup.
# CODE_STANDARDS § 2.4: "Never create files that were not needed."
/fix_*.py
/auto_fix_*.py
/revert_script.py
/specific_remover.py
/restore.py
/gen_batch*.ps1
/generate.ps1
/*_analyze.txt
/remaining_errors.txt
/missing_controllers.txt
# Analyzer / build output is reproducible; regenerate it, do not commit it.
/full_analyze.txt
/*.log
